%description:

Tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass:dctcp -- DCTCP sends its ECN-setup SYN with ECT.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "the NED type of the algorithm of each connection"; DcTcp is RFC 8257,
which needs ECN on each packet, also on the SYN, as Linux.

The classes: the nine algorithms of the package inet.transportlayer.tcp.flavours (TcpNewReno is
the default, TcpReno the value of LegacyTcp, TcpCubic of LinuxTcp), qualified-alias (a
qualified NED type name), unknown (a name without a NED type: an error) and socket-override
(the open command of a socket selects another algorithm). Each test of an algorithm checks a
behavior that none of the other eight algorithms shows. The code:
TcpConnection::createTcpAlgorithm() and the classes of the algorithms. This test separates DcTcp: the eight others send the SYN Not-ECT (RFC 3168 section 6.1.1).

Host under test: host1, with tcpEcnMode "rfc3168" (a precondition), which sends 100000 bytes.
Observation: level 1, the SYN of host1 arrives at host2 with ECT(0).

%# parameter: Tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass
%# class: dctcp
%# value: "DcTcp"
%# key: *.host1.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpAlgorithmClassDcTcp")
        .once(on("host2.eth[0].mac").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("ipv4.ecn == 2")
             .describe("the SYN of host1 at host2, with ECT(0)")
             .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpAlgorithmClassDcTcp compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.tcpAlgorithmClass = "DcTcp"    # the parameter under test
*.linkDelay = 10ms
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100000B
*.host1.tcp.tcpEcnMode = "rfc3168"
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpAlgorithmClassDcTcp: PASS
